## Invariants (non-negotiable)

- The quality gate is strict: no warnings-only mode. A diagnostic is either an
  error or suppressed with a documented, tracked rationale.
- **Tests are realistic, not mocked, and complete, not minimal.** E2E drives the
  real built artifact across real boundaries; a feature is not done until a real
  journey covers it. (See "Tests are context engineering" — this is a rule, not a
  preference.)
- Validate all external / IO inputs at trust boundaries.
- Keep the artifact portable across the supported platform matrix.
- Do not commit secrets, credentials, PII, or customer data.

## Scripts and output are context

- Every script you add should be quiet on success — a single line or nothing.
- On failure, print the exact error and a concrete suggested next action.
- Treat all command output as context the next agent has to read: maximize
  signal, minimize noise.

## Tests are context engineering

This is an agent-driven repo: the test suite is the *only* QA loop — no human
clicks through the features. So tests are not a chore that trails the work; they
are how you and the next agent see whether the system actually works. Optimize
for realism and coverage, never for speed or a green checkmark. Realism here is a
rule, not a preference (see Invariants).

- **Never mock the thing you are trying to prove.** An "e2e" test that mocks the
  network, filesystem, subprocess, or the artifact's own entry point proves the
  mock, not the product — that is the fast-and-mocked failure mode, not the goal.
  Drive the *real* built artifact across the *real* boundary the way a user does:
  invoke the installed CLI as a subprocess and assert on exit code/stdout/stderr;
  hit a real local server or DB; read and write real files in a temp dir. Mock
  only a genuinely external third party you cannot run locally, and say which.
- **A green mocked suite is worse than no suite.** It manufactures false
  confidence the next agent builds on, and the breakage surfaces in production
  where no human is watching.
- **"Done" means complete, not minimal.** Cover *every* user-facing journey — the
  happy path **and** the meaningful failure/recovery paths — not one smoke test.
  A feature is not finished until a real e2e journey exercises it end to end.
- **Coverage is a floor, not the target.** The line-coverage gate is satisfiable
  with mocks that touch lines without proving behavior; clearing it is not the
  same as realistic coverage. Pursue real journeys and let the number follow.
- **Keep the journey list below current.** When you add or change a feature, add
  or update its journey here and in the e2e suite, so coverage is auditable and
  grows with the repo.

Covered e2e journeys:

- <journey 1: a user runs ... and gets ...>
- <journey 2: on bad input / failure X, the tool ... and recovers/exits ...>

## Keeping the allowlist current

- The agent command allowlist lives in `.claude/settings.json`; the tool
  enforces it, so this file does not restate "follow the allowlist."
- Your job is to keep it current: when a new routine command becomes part of the
  normal build/test/release workflow, add it to the allowlist instead of
  re-approving it every session. Keep it narrow — prefer allowlists over deny
  lists, and leave dangerous operations out unless they are genuinely required.

